latest_plan = "Based on the reflection, here's a detailed step-by-step plan to solve the challenge:\n\n1. Identify Sky Blue Shapes:\n   a. Use flood fill algorithm to identify all sky blue (8) shapes in the grid.\n   b. Store each shape as a set of coordinates.\n\n2. Create Initial Shadow Map:\n   a. Initialize a 2D array of the same size as the input grid, filled with zeros.\n   b. This will store shadow intensity values.\n\n3. Generate Base Shadow for Each Shape:\n   a. For each sky blue shape:\n      i. Create a halo around the shape, maintaining a one-pixel buffer.\n      ii. Set initial intensity values, higher for pixels closer to the shape.\n      iii. Increase intensity for bottom and right sides of the shape.\n      iv. Extend the shadow further in the bottom-right direction.\n\n4. Apply Distance-based Intensity:\n   a. For each pixel in the shadow map:\n      i. Calculate its distance from the nearest sky blue pixel.\n      ii. Adjust its intensity based on this distance (higher intensity for closer pixels).\n\n5. Create Bridges Between Shapes:\n   a. If multiple shapes exist:\n      i. Identify the closest points between each pair of shapes.\n      ii. Create a path of yellow pixels between these points, favoring bottom-right direction.\n      iii. Gradually decrease intensity along the path.\n\n6. Add Scattered Shadow Pixels:\n   a. In areas of black space:\n      i. Add random yellow pixels, with higher probability towards bottom-right.\n      ii. Ensure these pixels maintain the one-pixel buffer from sky blue.\n      iii. Set lower intensity for these scattered pixels.\n\n7. Refine Shadow Edges:\n   a. Introduce slight randomness to shadow edges to avoid straight lines.\n   b. Ensure continuity of shadow paths, especially in bridges between shapes.\n\n8. Scale Shadow Complexity:\n   a. For larger sky blue shapes, increase the complexity of the shadow:\n      i. Add more detail to the shadow edges.\n      ii. Increase the range of the shadow effect.\n\n9. Balance Shape Contours and Directionality:\n   a. Adjust shadow pixels to partially follow the contours of sky blue shapes.\n   b. Maintain overall bottom-right directionality of the shadow.\n\n10. Handle Grid Edge Cases:\n    a. For shapes near grid edges, compress shadows on the edge side.\n    b. Compensate by intensifying shadows on visible sides.\n\n11. Convert Shadow Map to Yellow Pixels:\n    a. For each non-zero value in the shadow map:\n       i. If the intensity is above a certain threshold, set the corresponding pixel in the output grid to yellow (4).\n       ii. Ensure no yellow pixel directly touches a sky blue pixel.\n\n12. Final Cleanup:\n    a. Remove any isolated yellow pixels that aren't part of a larger shadow structure.\n    b. Ensure all yellow pixels have at least one other yellow pixel adjacent (including diagonally).\n\n13. Return the Modified Grid:\n    a. The original grid with added yellow shadow pixels represents the final output.\n\nThis plan incorporates all the key observations and should produce consistent results across various input cases, adhering to the patterns observed in the examples while allowing for the necessary flexibility to handle new scenarios."
